.TH VAULT-LIST 1 "October 2026" "vault" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vault-list, vault-ls \- list files and folders in the vault
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vault ls
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-\-key
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-vault
.IR DIR ]
.RI [ FOLDER ]
.br
.B vault list
.RB [ \-\-key
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-vault
.IR DIR ]
.RI [ FOLDER ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Both print one line per entry: human-readable size, modification time
(local, minute resolution) and name, in fixed-width columns so the name
always starts at character 31.
.PP
.B vault ls
shows one level, like
.BR ls (1):
each sub-folder of
.I FOLDER
(the top of the vault if none is given) once, with a trailing
.BR / ,
and the files directly in it.
A folder's size is the total of everything under it and its time is the
newest among them. Names are relative to
.IR FOLDER ,
so
.B vault ls photos
then
.B vault ls photos/2025
walks down the tree. Folders are listed first, then files, each sorted by
name. If
.I FOLDER
is a file's name, that file alone is shown.
.PP
.B vault list
shows every file at or under
.IR FOLDER ,
recursively, with full names, sorted. It is the form to pipe into other
commands, and
.B vault ls \-r
is the same.
.PP
Records the passphrase cannot open are skipped silently. Takes a shared lock.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-r
.BR ls :
list everything under
.I FOLDER
with full names, as
.B vault list
does.
.TP
.BI \-\-key " PATH"
Key file. Default: $VAULT_KEY, else ~/.vault/key.
.TP
.BI \-\-vault " DIR"
Vault directory. Default: $VAULT_DIR, else ~/.vault/store.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VAULT_PASSPHRASE
Passphrase, instead of prompting on the terminal.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success. 1 if
.B vault ls
is given a
.I FOLDER
with nothing in or under it, or on any other error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
vault ls
.br
vault ls photos/2025
.br
vault list | cut \-c31\-        # every name, one per line
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vault (1),
.BR vault\-get (1),
.BR vault\-rm (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Andy Dixon <vault@dixon.cx>, https://www.dixon.cx
